{"custom_id": "6921e28f6325061b53a95f8a40967430dcc5110ece6f0ef8", "meta": {"loop": "T-r02-type-3600793", "task": "annotate_with_guidelines", "occurrence": 0, "attempt": 0, "max_tokens": 128000, "prompt_sha256": "aefaf8ab3a6d1b06deb17b7c52e7eb28d7036b3078550f0b9074aa791c944662", "prompt_chars": 11219}, "batch_id": "msgbatch_017fwLpZ2N5PTU9aMFy9gJ29", "saved_utc": "2026-11-13T22:26:43+00:00", "message": {"id": "msg_011Ch1i9jp2kvKWJJ7uMUjZD", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "I'm going through the candidates one at a time. The first is \"Duchenne muscular dystrophy\" from the cDNA mapping sentence, which I'm classing as a SpecificDisease under section 1. I'm now starting on candidate 2, the same disease name appearing with its abbreviation in parentheses.\n\nNext I'm weighing \"disease\" at 408-415 as a generic term in \"this genetic disease,\" which I'm marking NONE under the what-not-to-annotate rule. Then \"muscular dystrophy\" at 518-536, in \"showing muscular dystrophy,\" reads as a family of diseases rather than a modifier, so it's a DiseaseClass under section 1. I'm still deciding whether the mdx mouse mutant name counts as a disease at all.\n\nI'm now weighing whether \"mdx\" counts as a disease mention at all, since it names a mouse mutant strain rather than a disease. Its phrasing alongside \"mutation\" and \"pathological features\" makes it unclear whether it's used as a modifier or as a standalone disease reference.\n\nI'm settling whether \"mdx\" counts as a disease mention. It reads as the name of a mouse mutant strain, a model of the disease rather than the disease itself, and the guidelines only annotate organism or strain names when the disease they cause is being discussed. I'm weighing a NONE label for that reason, though it still refers to the muscular dystrophy condition, so it's a close call.\n\nI'm leaning toward NONE for \"mdx\" here, since it names a mouse mutant strain rather than a disease, though it's still a close call because the strain is a model of the muscular dystrophy condition. Then I'm checking whether the same reasoning carries over to its other mentions, where it's used as a modifier of \"mutation\" or to describe the condition's pathological features.\n\nI'm checking the remaining candidates against the same rules: the mdx references, the Becker and Emery muscular dystrophy names, and the DMD mentions, including whether DMD is a standalone disease or a modifier of cDNA clone.\n\nI'm checking whether \"DMD\" in \"the DMD locus\" counts as a disease mention or just a gene symbol. The abbreviation was already tagged as the disease earlier, but this instance refers to the gene rather than the condition, so I'm weighing whether gene-name usage should be excluded even though the string is the same.\n\nI'm weighing whether \"DMD\" used for the gene locus should count as a disease mention, since the string matches the disease abbreviation. Where it modifies a noun like \"cDNA clone\" or \"locus,\" I'd treat it as a Modifier, but where it stands alone as the linkage target, it reads more like a standalone disease reference.\n\nI'm now assigning labels to the remaining DMD mentions: \"DMD locus\" and \"DMD cDNA clone\" are modifiers of their nouns, while the standalone \"linked to DMD\" reference counts as a specific disease. I'm also reconsidering candidate 1, where Duchenne muscular dystrophy modifies \"gene\" and so gets labeled as a modifier.\n\nI'm treating the \"Duchenne muscular dystrophy ( DMD )\" phrase in \"the Duchenne muscular dystrophy ( DMD ) gene\" as a modifier of \"gene,\" since the disease name sits in front of a noun. I'm weighing whether the parenthetical abbreviation should be marked the same way, given how the guidelines handle abbreviation definitions.\n\nI'm reconsidering the modifier cases here: \"Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) gene\" uses the disease name to modify \"gene\", so I'm leaning toward Modifier for both the spelled-out name and its abbreviation. Next is \"Becker muscular dystrophy type\", where the disease name modifies \"type\", so I'd also mark it Modifier, though it's a specific disease.\n\nI'm now going through the rest of the candidates: Emery Dreifuss muscular dystrophy is a standalone specific disease, and \"disease\" by itself is a generic term I'm leaving unannotated. \"Muscular dystrophy\" as a family of diseases counts as a disease class, and I'm about to work through the mdx mentions.\n\nI'm weighing \"mdx\" on its own: it names a mouse mutant strain, so I'd lean NONE for the appositive, though its pathological features and its linkage to G6PD and Hprt may point to the disease itself.\n\nI'm settling on SpecificDisease for the mdx mentions that name the mutant and its pathology, and Modifier where mdx is used to describe the mutation.\n\nI'm treating the \"mdx\" mouse strain as a disease mention after all, since the muscular dystrophy it causes is what's being discussed. So the mdx mutant in the appositive gets SpecificDisease, and its pathological features do too. Now I'm moving on to the \"DMD locus\" reference.\n\nI'm now checking the DMD mention in \"closely linked to DMD on the human X chromosome,\" which reads as a standalone disease reference, so I'm labeling it SpecificDisease. Candidates 1, 2, and 3 all use the disease name or abbreviation to modify \"gene,\" so I'm labeling them Modifier, with the parenthetical abbreviation in candidate 3 handled the same way.\n\nI'm settling the labels for the last few candidates: the Becker name modifies \"type,\" so it's a Modifier, while Emery Dreifuss muscular dystrophy stands alone and counts as a specific disease. I'm also marking the muscular dystrophy family as a disease class and giving the first three candidates Modifier labels, since each uses the disease name or its abbreviation to modify \"gene.\"\n\nI'm settling the labels for the remaining mdx mentions and the Becker and Emery names, with the family-level muscular dystrophy term marked as a disease class.\n\nI'm settling the last few labels: \"DMD\" standing alone as the linkage target counts as a specific disease, while \"DMD cDNA clone\" and \"mdx mutation\" use the name to modify a noun, so those are modifiers.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "{\"decisions\":[{\"id\":1,\"entity\":\"Modifier\",\"rationale\":\"'Duchenne muscular dystrophy' is used as a modifier of the noun 'gene' in 'the human X-linked Duchenne muscular dystrophy gene', so the disease name is annotated as a Modifier rather than a standalone disease.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 3: Annotate disease mentions that are used as Modifiers for other concepts\"},{\"id\":2,\"entity\":\"Modifier\",\"rationale\":\"In 'the Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) gene', the disease name modifies the noun 'gene', so it is a Modifier.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 3: Annotate disease mentions that are used as Modifiers for other concepts\"},{\"id\":3,\"entity\":\"Modifier\",\"rationale\":\"'DMD' is the abbreviation defined by the parenthetical after the long form; it is a separate synonymous mention and, like the long form, serves as a modifier of 'gene'.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 6: Annotate all synonymous mentions; What to Annotate 3: Modifiers\"},{\"id\":4,\"entity\":\"NONE\",\"rationale\":\"'disease' in 'this genetic disease' is a very general term and is excluded.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What NOT to Annotate 4: Do not annotate general terms\"},{\"id\":5,\"entity\":\"DiseaseClass\",\"rationale\":\"'muscular dystrophy' denotes a family of multiple specific diseases, so it is a Disease Class mention.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 1: Disease Class\"},{\"id\":6,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'mdx' names the mouse mutant that displays a specific muscular dystrophy phenotype; the disease caused by it is clearly being discussed, so it is annotated as a Specific Disease.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What NOT to Annotate 1: organism exception when the disease is discussed; What to Annotate 1: Specific Disease\"},{\"id\":7,\"entity\":\"Modifier\",\"rationale\":\"'DMD' in 'the DMD locus' modifies the noun 'locus', so it is a Modifier.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 3: Annotate disease mentions that are used as Modifiers for other concepts\"},{\"id\":8,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'the pathological features of mdx' refers to the specific disease condition of mdx as a standalone noun phrase, so it is a Specific Disease.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 1: Specific Disease\"},{\"id\":9,\"entity\":\"Modifier\",\"rationale\":\"'the Becker muscular dystrophy type' uses the disease name as a modifier of the noun 'type', so it is a Modifier.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 3: Annotate disease mentions that are used as Modifiers for other concepts\"},{\"id\":10,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'linkage of mdx to G6PD' uses mdx as a standalone reference to the specific mutant/disease rather than modifying a noun, so it is a Specific Disease.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 1: Specific Disease\"},{\"id\":11,\"entity\":\"Modifier\",\"rationale\":\"'the mdx mutation' uses 'mdx' as a modifier of the noun 'mutation', so it is a Modifier.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 3: Annotate disease mentions that are used as Modifiers for other concepts\"},{\"id\":12,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'Emery Dreifuss muscular dystrophy' is a single, specific disease with no further categorization, used as a standalone noun phrase.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 1: Specific Disease\"},{\"id\":13,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'linked to DMD on the human X chromosome' uses DMD as a standalone disease reference, not as a modifier of a noun, so it is a Specific Disease.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 1: Specific Disease\"},{\"id\":14,\"entity\":\"Modifier\",\"rationale\":\"'the mdx mutation' uses 'mdx' as a modifier of the noun 'mutation', so it is a Modifier.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 3: Annotate disease mentions that are used as Modifiers for other concepts\"},{\"id\":15,\"entity\":\"Modifier\",\"rationale\":\"'a DMD cDNA clone' uses DMD as a modifier of the noun phrase 'cDNA clone', so it is a Modifier.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 3: Annotate disease mentions that are used as Modifiers for other concepts\"}]}", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4805, "output_tokens": 8609, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 6998}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}